INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant: 3CX Software Supply Chain Compromise Initiated by a Prior Software Supply Chain Compromise", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/3cx-software-supply-chain-compromise", "text": "SRC-003 findings: Mandiant says the initial compromise vector of 3CX's network was malicious software downloaded from the Trading Technologies website. The earlier compromise involved a tampered X_TRADER installer. A 3CX employee later installed the infected application, allowing access to spread through 3CX's network, reach the software-development/build server, and corrupt the 3CX installer. Mandiant identified the 3CX campaign as a cascading software supply-chain compromise. In the 3CX software, versions 18.12.416 and earlier contained SUDDENICON, which obtained C2 servers from encrypted icon files hosted on GitHub, then downloaded ICONICSTEALER, a browser-information dataminer. For propagation/build compromise, Mandiant says the attacker used a compiled Fast Reverse Proxy project to move laterally; harvested credentials; compromised both Windows and macOS build environments. Windows: TAXHAUL launcher and COLDCAT downloader persisted via DLL search-order hijacking through IKEEXT with LocalSystem privileges. macOS: POOLRAT backdoor persisted via Launch Daemons; prior reporting's SIMPLESEA identification was revised to POOLRAT. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster. It assesses with moderate confidence that UNC4736 is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity, citing overlap with Trading Technologies compromise and POOLRAT associated with trojanized CoinGoTrade. SRC-003 also says the supply-chain compromise is the first known cascading example and that UNC4736 is most likely linked to financially motivated North Korean operators."}
